    description: "Coupled deliverables that must land together because they're structurally inseparable: reclassifying ADR-0025/0026 makes ADR-0024's text wrong; introducing the audit-findings format requires a methodology home and a filing rubric. Splitting would create temporary inconsistency windows.\n\n# Background — the categorical confusion\n\nADR-0023 (Edge.edge_type axis principle) and ADR-0024 (axis-declaration meta-template) fit the canonical ADR mold: each is a single load-bearing decision with consequences. ADR-0025 and ADR-0026, by contrast, are largely TABLES OF PER-VALUE VERDICTS produced by applying the Fundamental Concept Audit playbook to specific families (dispatch+publish, IPC). Read literally, ADR-0025 is 13 verdicts arrived at by the four-leakage-test methodology; ADR-0026 is 7 verdicts in the same shape. The \"decision\" in each isn't an architectural principle — it's a case ruling under existing law (ADR-0023 + ADR-0024). They're better classified as audit-findings documents.\n\nADR-0025 itself acknowledges this categorically: \"ADR-0024's axis-declaration template is the abstract shape; this ADR is a per-family classification within an existing axis (Edge.edge_type), not a new axis declaration.\" That sentence is the doc admitting it's not principle-shaped.\n\nThis codebase has been comfortable treating \"any structurally-significant document with consequences for the code\" as ADR-worthy. That's a permissive definition that produces audit-finding documents with verdict tables (ADR-0025, ADR-0026) being filed alongside genuine principles (ADR-0023, ADR-0024). The trajectory matters: ADR-0027 (Symbol.kind axis) and ADR-0028 (evidence_type axis) are predicted to trigger 2-6 audit-findings follow-ons each (per the WI-dumiz-bikul / WI-turin-pajuk audit verdicts). If those ship under the ADR namespace, the ADR list will dilute further — discoverability suffers because a reader trying to understand \"what principles govern this codebase\" gets a noisy mix of principles and rulings. Doing this cleanup AFTER 6+ more ADR-0025-shape entries land will be substantially more painful than doing it now.\n\n# Settled design decisions\n\nThe following are NOT open questions; they're decisions taken before this item's creation, recorded here so they survive into PR-drafting:\n\n1. **Meta-key discipline integrates into ADR-0024** as a new sub-section (integrated, not follow-up ADR; not in ADR-0023 because that ADR is in-flight migration). It operationalizes ADR-0023's \"or a dedicated `Edge` field, when warranted\" clause via three rules:\n   - **Endpoint-property check**: if a property is queryable from src or dst, query the endpoint, don't put it in `meta`.\n   - **Axis-leakage check**: if a property describes a different declared (or pending-declaration) axis, route to that axis even if the axis isn't formally declared yet.\n   - **Recurrence-promotion threshold**: if a meta key recurs across N edges or N producers, promote to a dedicated `Edge` field. (N to be set during ADR-0024 drafting; default candidates: ≥3 edge_type values share the meta key, OR ≥2 producer modules emit it.)\n\n2. **CANONICAL/FOLD/DEPRECATE-NO-FOLD verdict methodology lifts into ADR-0024** as a new sub-section. Reasoning: it's general — every axis-conformance audit produces verdicts in this shape — so it belongs at the axis-template abstraction level, not duplicated in every audit-findings doc. Explicit refactor trigger: \"if future axis declarations (ADR-0027/0028) reveal that the verdict scheme genuinely varies per axis, lift to `docs/family-audit-methodology.md` at that point — don't speculatively split now.\"\n\n3. **Audit-findings format is hybrid YAML-in-fenced-block within markdown.** Each audit-findings doc is a single .md file; the verdicts table is replaced by a fenced ```yaml ... ``` block. The property test extracts the YAML block and validates each verdict's Status field against codebase reality. Markdown narrative (Context, Methodology pointer, Diagnostic findings, Migration impact, etc.) lives outside the YAML block.\n\n4. **Numbering: new audit-findings series renumbers from 0001.** Stubs at the old `docs/adr/0025-...md` and `docs/adr/0026-...md` paths say \"ADR-0025 was reclassified as audit-findings 0001\" (and analogous for 0026 → 0002), so verbal/written citations of the old identifiers retain a discoverable handle. Future audit-findings docs grow the new series independently of ADR numbers, avoiding ADR-0027-collision risk.\n\n5. **Filing rubric is positive/expansive (defines valid shapes), not restrictive.** Long-Context ADRs are a valid bucket-1 shape, NOT mis-classification. Bucket boundary is \"decision present?\" — if yes, ADR (regardless of Context length); if no and per-value verdicts, audit-findings; if no and inventory/snapshot, survey. ADR-0003 lineage docs (original, usage-context-patterns, linker-subcategory-restoration) all have decisions and stay in `docs/adr/`. No grandfathering needed; no follow-up reclassification item required. (Spot-check during drafting: skim each of the three to confirm \"what's the decision?\" has a clean answer.)\n\n6. **Three-state lifecycle for verdict rows**: UNRESOLVED / PRELIM_RESOLVED / RESOLVED, with mechanical-check predicates enforced by property test. NOT four-state — bakeoff validation is orthogonal and tracked via the `awaits_bakeoff_validation` tag on the migration's tracker item; duplicating it in audit-findings would add no information.\n\n# Deliverable 1: Reclassify ADR-0025 + ADR-0026 to docs/audits/\n\n1.1. **Create** `docs/audits/` directory.\n\n1.2. **Relocate content**:\n   - ADR-0025 → `docs/audits/0001-dispatch-publish-family.md`\n   - ADR-0026 → `docs/audits/0002-ipc-family.md`\n\n1.3. **Lift methodology section out of each relocated doc.** The CANONICAL/FOLD/DEPRECATE-NO-FOLD trichotomy currently duplicated in both docs gets removed and replaced with a one-paragraph pointer to ADR-0024's new methodology sub-section. The relocated docs become thin: Context (specific to this family), pointer to ADR-0024 for methodology, the YAML block of verdicts with lifecycle states, family-specific diagnostic findings, family-specific migration impact.\n\n1.4. **Reformat verdicts as YAML-in-fenced-block.** Replace the existing markdown verdict tables in each doc with a fenced YAML block. Each verdict row carries: `value`, `verdict`, `fold_target` (nullable), `status`, `diagnostic_test`, `rationale`. Existing prose (Migration impact, Diagnostic findings worth naming, etc.) stays as markdown alongside.\n\n1.5. **Initial Status values for relocated docs**: all rows = `RESOLVED` (Phase 4b at SCHEMA_VERSION 0.4.0 cleared every value in both audits' scope).\n\n1.6. **Stub at old paths**: `docs/adr/0025-...md` and `docs/adr/0026-...md` are kept as redirect stubs with the agreed wording: \"RELOCATED — ADR-0025 was reclassified as audit-findings 0001 because it's an audit-findings document (per-value verdicts under existing law from ADR-0023), not an architecture decision. See `docs/audits/0001-dispatch-publish-family.md`.\" Stubs are permanent, not date-expiring.\n\n# Deliverable 2: Revise ADR-0024 substantively\n\n2.1. **Workflow-language revision throughout the seven-step description.** Wherever a step's output is described as \"a follow-on ADR\" or similar (especially around per-family audits during Phase 3), change to \"an audit-findings document at `docs/audits/<NN>-<topic>.md` citing the parent ADR.\" The axis-declaration ADR itself is one document; per-family verdicts that fall out during migration are separate documents in a different series.\n\n2.2. **New sub-section: Family-audit verdict methodology.** Lifts the CANONICAL/FOLD/DEPRECATE-NO-FOLD trichotomy from ADR-0025/0026. Establishes that it applies to any axis-conformance audit, not just Edge.edge_type's families. Includes the explicit refactor trigger (\"if future axis declarations reveal the verdict scheme varies per axis, lift to a separate doc at that point\").\n\n2.3. **New sub-section: Fold-residue discipline.** Operationalizes ADR-0023's \"or a dedicated `Edge` field, when warranted\" clause via the three rules in Settled Decisions §1. Names where meta-key audits get triggered (cadence-hook periodic pass + per-axis-migration review). Names the recurrence-promotion threshold N (decided during drafting).\n\n2.4. **§ Enforcement update.** Family-audit outputs file as audit-findings documents, not as new ADRs. Cite the relocated `docs/audits/0001-...md` and `docs/audits/0002-...md` as worked examples of the audit-findings outputs that the seven-step workflow tends to produce. Distinguish: ADR-0023 = the axis-declaration ADR (Step 1-2 output); the audits = the family-verdict documents (Step 5-6 output during Phase 3 migration).\n\n2.5. **Worked-example pointer extension.** ADR-0024 currently cites only ADR-0023 as the worked example. Add the relocated audit-findings docs as worked examples of the methodology output.\n\n2.6. **Status section.** Confirm Accepted (or change from Draft to Accepted if appropriate per the audit-findings format landing). Add an \"updated YYYY-MM-DD\" footnote noting the audit-findings filing-path clarification, the verdict methodology lift, and the fold-residue discipline addition. Don't supersede the ADR; just refine.\n\n# Deliverable 3: Audit-findings format spec\n\n3.1. **Sibling `docs/audits/README.md`.** Establishes the format for the new series:\n   - File naming: `<NN>-<topic>.md` with NN starting at 0001\n   - Required sections: Context, Methodology pointer (typically to ADR-0024), Verdicts (YAML block), Diagnostic findings (optional), Migration impact (optional), Related (cross-references)\n   - YAML block schema: array of objects with `value`, `verdict`, `fold_target`, `status`, `diagnostic_test`, `rationale`\n   - Three-state lifecycle definition: UNRESOLVED / PRELIM_RESOLVED / RESOLVED with mechanical-check predicates\n   - Maintenance: hand-edit-with-validation; the property test enforces consistency\n\n3.2. **Three-state lifecycle definitions and mechanical-check predicates:**\n   - **UNRESOLVED**: verdict recorded; producers still emit the value. ↔ value is in registry as endpoint_shape AND production emit sites exist.\n   - **PRELIM_RESOLVED**: producer migration shipped; value remains in registry through the deprecation window. ↔ value is in registry as endpoint_shape AND zero production emit sites.\n   - **RESOLVED**: registry pruned; consumer-side enumerations cleaned up. ↔ value is absent from registry.\n\n   For DEPRECATE-NO-FOLD verdicts (e.g., `event_subscribes`), the lifecycle compresses: the producer drop is the migration (UNRESOLVED → PRELIM_RESOLVED), then registry removal is RESOLVED. Same three states apply.\n\n3.3. **Property test.** New test in the audit-findings package (or hypergumbo-core's tests, depending on placement) that:\n   - Walks `docs/audits/*.md`\n   - Extracts each fenced YAML block tagged as the verdicts block (some sentinel — e.g., a stable section heading \"## Verdicts\" preceding the block, or a YAML key like `kind: audit_verdicts` inside it)\n   - For each verdict row, evaluates the mechanical-check predicate matching its Status\n   - Fails if any row's Status disagrees with codebase reality\n   - Same shape as schema-freshness and concept-axes-freshness pre-commit checks\n\n3.4. **Pre-commit wiring.** The property test runs on pre-commit when audit-findings docs OR registry files OR producer source files change. Skips otherwise.\n\n3.5. **Bakeoff-validation orthogonality note.** `docs/audits/README.md` explicitly states that bakeoff validation is tracked separately via the `awaits_bakeoff_validation` tag on the migration's tracker item, NOT in the audit-findings doc. Single source of truth for that orthogonal concern.\n\n# Deliverable 4: Filing-convention guidance\n\n4.1. **`docs/adr/README.md`** gains a \"When to write an ADR vs an audit-findings document vs a survey/snapshot\" section (positive/expansive rubric, NOT restrictive):\n\n   - **Bucket 1 — ADR (`docs/adr/<NN>-<topic>.md`)**: a load-bearing decision document. May have a substantial Context section that includes analysis, surveys, or comparisons of alternatives — those serve the decision. Use even when Context is long, as long as the deliverable is fundamentally a decision. The decision is the deliverable; the analysis is in service of it. Examples: ADR-0023 (principle), ADR-0024 (template), ADR-0003 lineage (decisions with substantial Context).\n   - **Bucket 2 — Audit-findings (`docs/audits/<NN>-<topic>.md`)**: a record of per-value verdicts produced by applying an existing methodology (typically Fundamental Concept Audit) to a specific scope. Records case rulings under existing law, NOT new principles. Has lifecycle states + structured YAML format per `docs/audits/README.md`. Examples: audit-findings 0001 (dispatch+publish family), audit-findings 0002 (IPC family).\n   - **Bucket 3 — Survey/snapshot (`docs/surveys/<topic>.md` or `docs/architecture-snapshots/<date>.md`)**: a catalog or point-in-time inventory with no associated decision. Pure descriptive. Use when neither a decision nor a verdict-set applies.\n\n   **Bucket boundary**: decision present? → bucket 1, regardless of Context length. No decision; per-value verdicts? → bucket 2. No decision; inventory/catalog? → bucket 3.\n\n   **Bucket-1 self-justification guard**: Bucket 1 requires a load-bearing decision (a principle adopted, a template defined, an architectural change committed to). Context may be substantial in service of the decision, but the decision is the deliverable. If the deliverable is fundamentally a description with a \"we should...\" paragraph at the end, that's bucket 3 with weak decision content, not bucket 1.\n\n4.2. **AGENTS.md** gains a one-paragraph essentialization in the appropriate section (Architecture & Context, or Required Checks):\n\n   \"ADR vs audit-findings filing convention: load-bearing decision documents go in `docs/adr/`; per-value verdict tables produced by applying existing methodology to a scope go in `docs/audits/` (see `docs/audits/README.md`); pure inventories/snapshots go in `docs/surveys/` or similar. The bucket boundary is 'decision present?' — see `docs/adr/README.md` for the full rubric.\"\n\n4.3. **Audit playbook Step 6 update** (`.agent/agent_playbooks_protocols_sops_skills/what-if-we-dont-know-what-the-fuck-we-are-talking-about-audit-aka-fundamental-concept-audit.md`):\n\n   Step 6 currently is \"deprecate, document, or keep\" — that's a different abstraction level than the audit verdict trichotomy. Revise Step 6 to:\n   - Specify WHERE the audit's outcome is documented: `docs/audits/<NN>-<topic>.md` for per-value verdicts; ADR if the audit produced a new principle.\n   - Cite ADR-0024's verdict methodology section (the new home of CANONICAL/FOLD/DEPRECATE-NO-FOLD) for the actual scheme.\n   - Cite `docs/audits/README.md` for the format.\n   - Note that ADR-0025/0026 were originally filed as ADRs in error and have been reclassified.\n\n# Inbound-reference migration\n\nSearch-and-replace pattern across (verify before bulk-applying):\n\n- `docs/adr/0023-edge-type-relationship-not-endpoints.md` (multiple §6 references)\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/edge_types.py` (~12 EdgeTypeSpec.description strings)\n- `CHANGELOG.md` (multiple references in [Unreleased] and prior sections)\n- `docs/migrating-edge-types.md` (cites both in headings and prose)\n- `docs/adr/README.md` (index update; remove ADR-0025/0026 entries)\n- `docs/concept-axes.md` (auto-generated; will regenerate cleanly via `scripts/generate-concept-axes`)\n- `docs/blind-spots.md`\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/io_boundary.py`\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/linkers/*.py` (linker docstrings: ipc.py, tauri_ipc.py, router_routes.py, route_handler.py, phoenix_ipc.py, grpc.py, message_queue.py, yjs_crdt.py, di_resolution.py, message_dispatch.py, websocket.py, annotation_convention.py, event_sourcing.py)\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/test_*.py` (test docstrings: test_ipc_linker.py, test_event_sourcing_linker.py, test_ranking.py)\n- `packages/hypergumbo-lang-mainstream/src/hypergumbo_lang_mainstream/*.py` (cpp.py, ruby.py, c.py)\n- `packages/hypergumbo-lang-extended1/src/hypergumbo_lang_extended1/solidity.py`\n- Any other inbound surfaced by `git grep \"ADR-002[56]\" docs/adr/0025 docs/adr/0026`\n\nPer inbound: rewrite \"ADR-0025\" → \"audit-findings 0001\"; rewrite \"ADR-0026\" → \"audit-findings 0002\"; rewrite paths `docs/adr/0025-...md` → `docs/audits/0001-...md` and `docs/adr/0026-...md` → `docs/audits/0002-...md`. Stubs at old paths catch any missed references.\n\nVerify the registry's drift property test still passes (no expected impact; the relocation is pure documentation).\n\n# Acceptance criteria\n\n**A1. Relocation:**\n- `docs/audits/0001-dispatch-publish-family.md` and `docs/audits/0002-ipc-family.md` exist with reformatted content (YAML-in-fenced-block).\n- Methodology section removed from each; replaced with one-paragraph pointer to ADR-0024.\n- Stubs at `docs/adr/0025-...md` and `docs/adr/0026-...md` redirect with the agreed wording.\n\n**A2. ADR-0024 revision:**\n- Workflow-language updates throughout the seven-step description.\n- New sub-section for verdict methodology (CANONICAL/FOLD/DEPRECATE-NO-FOLD lifted in).\n- New sub-section for fold-residue discipline (three rules; threshold N specified).\n- § Enforcement updated to name audit-findings as the canonical Phase 3 / Phase 4 output.\n- Worked-example pointer extended to cite the relocated audit-findings docs.\n- Status section updated with \"updated YYYY-MM-DD\" footnote.\n\n**A3. Audit-findings format:**\n- `docs/audits/README.md` exists with format spec, three-state lifecycle definitions, mechanical-check predicates, and orthogonality-with-bakeoff-validation note.\n- Property test exists, passes, and runs on pre-commit when audit-findings docs OR registry files OR producer source files change.\n- Both relocated docs have all rows at Status: `RESOLVED` at relocation moment, and the property test confirms this.\n\n**A4. Filing rubric:**\n- `docs/adr/README.md` gains the positive/expansive bucket rubric with bucket-1 self-justification guard.\n- AGENTS.md gains the one-paragraph essentialization.\n- Audit playbook Step 6 revised to name filing path + cite ADR-0024 methodology + cite `docs/audits/README.md` format.\n\n**A5. Inbound references:**\n- All references to ADR-0025/0026 (textual identifier and path) updated across the codebase per the migration table.\n- Concept-axes doc regenerated cleanly.\n- Registry drift property test passes.\n- ADR index updated (ADR-0025/0026 entries removed; replaced by stub entries pointing at audit-findings 0001/0002, OR removed entirely if the index is considered a forward-looking artifact and stubs handle backward-discoverability).\n\n**A6. Spot-check ADR-0003 lineage**: brief check during rubric drafting that ADR-0003 (original), ADR-0003-ext (usage-context-patterns), and ADR-0003-ext (linker-subcategory-restoration) each contain a real decision (i.e., fit bucket 1). If any one of them turns out to be pure inventory, file separately as an exception (not in scope for this PR).\n\n**A7. ADR-0027/0028 readiness**: when ADR-0027 and ADR-0028 (the next axis declarations in flight per the HARD isbefore items) ship, their per-family verdict outputs file directly as `docs/audits/<NN>-<topic>.md`, NOT as new ADRs. The revised ADR-0024 templates the workflow that gets them filed correctly.\n\n# Dependencies\n\n**HARD isbefore**: WI-dahim-vupif-lavin-kukor-gufih-gutad-kanuv-johor (ADR-0027 draft) and WI-pilit-tokuh-nabij-fonin-sodis-salut-pibop-dirog (ADR-0028 draft). Reasoning: if those ADRs ship under the old \"any structurally-significant doc gets an ADR\" pattern, their per-family verdict follow-ons will likely cement the pattern further, making this cleanup substantially more painful. The revised ADR-0024 (in this item) is also the template ADR-0027/0028 will instantiate, so its template needs to be coherent before they ship.\n\n**Governance**: AGENTS.md edit + `.agent/agent_playbooks_protocols_sops_skills/...` edit triggers the AGENTS.md governance approval workflow per AGENTS.md's \"Governance Files\" rule. Status `needs_human_review` until human approval is recorded; PR drafted only after approval, via `auto-pr` (not self-merged).\n\n# Out of scope\n\n(Filed separately if the human or a future agent finds a compelling need.)\n\n- Reclassification of ADR-0003 lineage docs: NOT in scope and NOT needed under the positive/expansive rubric (per Settled Decisions §5). The rubric explicitly accommodates them as bucket-1 long-Context ADRs.\n- Renumbering scheme migration for the existing ADR series (e.g., shifting all post-0024 numbers down to absorb the freed 0025/0026 slots). Stubs at the old paths preserve discoverability without renumbering.\n- Pre-commit detection of new multi-value field additions on core dataclasses (currently relies on AGENTS.md prose; could be automated). Mentioned as ADR-0024 §3 open question but unrelated to this item's scope.\n- Retroactive lifecycle-state column for ADR-0015's dataflow access modes (which arguably constitute an axis predating ADR-0023). ADR-0024's existing open question (1) covers this."  # 7634
